WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.775
Hi, I'm Neymar O'Donnor, and this is Module 8 of OS

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:04.480
Work.

3
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.115
In Module 7, Denise built a restricted training bot, but it

4
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.455
only answers while her laptop is on.

5
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.835
In this last module, we'll move that bot off the laptop.

6
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.575
You'll understand what a VPS is, and how big it needs

7
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.595
to be, get into it over the internet without locking yourself

8
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.775
out, install only what's needed, close the front desk and the

9
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.615
password file, hand the bot to a caretaker that starts and

10
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.115
restarts it on its own, And finally, prove with a rehearsal

11
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.755
that your backup comes back whole.

12
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.815
You don't need to rent any machine right now, the practice

13
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.035
works on your own computer or on paper.

14
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.395
When I ask, pause the video and do it right away.

15
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.195
Start with Denise's problem.

16
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.655
Her training bot answers on Telegram only while the laptop is

17
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:51.175
on.

18
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.955
At 6 in the evening, she closes the lid and the

19
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.355
bot stops answering.

20
00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.375
The way out is a VPS.

21
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.480
Think of a rented room.

22
00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.855
The building owner hands you the room with lights and a

23
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.115
door, and whatever happens inside is up to you.

24
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.675
A VPS works the same way.

25
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.395
It's a rented virtual server, with memory, disk and network, from

26
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.095
a company called the VPS provider.

27
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.835
It runs all the time, but users, updates and programs are

28
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:17.335
your job.

29
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.275
On the laptop, you were already taking care of all that

30
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.075
without noticing.

31
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.315
On the VPS, you do it on purpose.

32
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.700
Both work.

33
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.435
The VPS solves the closed-lead problem and, along with it, brings

34
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.820
a new responsibility.

35
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.280
So, how big does this machine need to be?

36
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.735
Start small, with a machine that fits the program it will

37
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.480
run.

38
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.235
A bot that calls an AI model through the API doesn't

39
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.755
need an expensive graphics card, because the model runs on the

40
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.880
AI provider's computers.

41
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.775
The VPS only sends the request and receives the answer.

42
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.555
Lucia almost rented a VPS with a graphics card for a

43
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.160
bot with the class's fictional grade averages.

44
00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.190
The card would sit idle and the bill would be high.

45
00:01:59.215 --> 00:02:01.830
Notice too that there are three separate bills.

46
00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:06.410
The VPS monthly fee, which arrives every month, whether the machine

47
00:02:06.435 --> 00:02:10.350
is working or idle, the extra disk space and the AI

48
00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:11.550
API.

49
00:02:11.575 --> 00:02:13.890
Paying for one doesn't pay for the others.

50
00:02:13.915 --> 00:02:15.790
It's the same logic as module one.

51
00:02:15.815 --> 00:02:19.670
Each access has its own bill and you write down the

52
00:02:19.695 --> 00:02:20.875
limit for each one.

53
00:02:21.455 --> 00:02:24.450
you rent, remember that a machine that's on doesn't mean a

54
00:02:24.475 --> 00:02:25.710
healthy service.

55
00:02:25.735 --> 00:02:28.950
The bot can stop in there without anyone noticing.

56
00:02:28.975 --> 00:02:33.530
So first decide what needs to run non-stop and who will

57
00:02:33.555 --> 00:02:35.330
check when something goes wrong.

58
00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:40.130
The course kit includes the file plan of VPS.md and its

59
00:02:40.155 --> 00:02:42.750
first part has six lines.

60
00:02:42.775 --> 00:02:47.890
The work, the technical owner, the VPS budget, the separate API

61
00:02:47.915 --> 00:02:52.350
budget, the system which in examples is Ubuntu, and the shutdown

62
00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:52.810
plan.

63
00:02:52.835 --> 00:02:56.250
For Denise, the last one took the longest, where the cancel

64
00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:59.070
button is and who can press it.

65
00:02:59.095 --> 00:03:01.830
Pause the video and fill in the six lines with a

66
00:03:01.855 --> 00:03:02.770
job of your own.

67
00:03:02.795 --> 00:03:06.250
Where data is missing, write to be defined and the name

68
00:03:06.275 --> 00:03:07.050
of whoever decides.

69
00:03:07.075 --> 00:03:10.750
Nothing gets rented and the plan holds no password or credit

70
00:03:10.775 --> 00:03:11.610
card.

71
00:03:11.635 --> 00:03:14.710
With the room rented, the first door is the entrance.

72
00:03:14.735 --> 00:03:18.610
You get into the VPS through SSH, which creates a protected

73
00:03:18.635 --> 00:03:20.670
connection to manage the machine.

74
00:03:20.695 --> 00:03:25.910
The command is ssh, followed by the user, the at sign

75
00:03:25.935 --> 00:03:28.130
and the VPS address.

76
00:03:28.155 --> 00:03:31.810
User and address are fields to replace, not real values, and

77
00:03:31.835 --> 00:03:34.390
the address shows up in the providers panel.

78
00:03:34.415 --> 00:03:37.510
Lúcia opened two terminal windows and got mixed up.

79
00:03:37.535 --> 00:03:39.970
One was the laptop, the other was the VPS.

80
00:03:39.995 --> 00:03:43.670
What fixed it was looking at the start of the line,

81
00:03:43.695 --> 00:03:47.330
which once you're in shows the user and the VPS name,

82
00:03:47.355 --> 00:03:50.390
and and running the pwd command, which tells you the folder

83
00:03:50.415 --> 00:03:51.290
you're in.

84
00:03:51.315 --> 00:03:54.155
So, here's a habit for life.

85
00:03:54.395 --> 00:03:57.270
Before any command, look at the start of the line.

86
00:03:57.295 --> 00:03:59.175
It tells you which machine you're on.

87
00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.655
To get in, use the public key registered the way the

88
00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.040
provider tells you.

89
00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.475
The private key never leaves your computer, and the provider's panel

90
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.475
walks you through creating it when you sign up.

91
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.555
On the first connection, SSH shows the machine's fingerprint and asks

92
00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.740
whether you want to continue.

93
00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:20.040
Then it's compared that fingerprint with the one the panel showed,

94
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:21.260
and only then typed yes.

95
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.320
That question comes up once per machine.

96
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.475
If it shows up again for the same VPS, stop and

97
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.360
investigate.

98
00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.680
Inside, work with a user of your own, the work user.

99
00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.755
When a task needs administrator permission, put sudo in front of

100
00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:36.675
the command.

101
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.715
That way, that power shows up only where you ask for

102
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:39.940
it.

103
00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.100
And when you type the password, nothing appears on the screen.

104
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:45.920
That's normal.

105
00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.720
Now, the rule that gives this lesson its name.

106
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.035
Don't return the old key before testing the new one.

107
00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.475
Before you change the network or the way you get in,

108
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.040
leave the original session open.

109
00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.675
Open another window and test the new entry.

110
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.175
Only close the first one when the second one works.

111
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.515
And check beforehand that the recovery console in the providers panel

112
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.289
opens the machine if everything fails.

113
00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:13.804
Pause the video and analyze a case on paper.

114
00:05:13.829 --> 00:05:18.064
Rogério, Denise's coworker, pasted a command from the internet that

115
00:05:18.089 --> 00:05:21.204
changed the SSH port, ran it and closed the terminal right

116
00:05:21.229 --> 00:05:21.944
away.

117
00:05:21.969 --> 00:05:25.244
Now he can't get back in, and he never opened the

118
00:05:25.269 --> 00:05:25.724
console.

119
00:05:25.749 --> 00:05:27.164
Answer three questions.

120
00:05:27.189 --> 00:05:31.484
Which rule was missing, where he can still get in, and

121
00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:33.509
what he writes in the plan.

122
00:05:33.609 --> 00:05:35.104
Then check the lesson's answer key.

123
00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:39.204
Inside, the machine comes almost empty, and the question becomes,

124
00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:40.144
what to put in it?

125
00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:44.624
The course examples use Ubuntu, which installs and updates programs

126
00:05:44.649 --> 00:05:45.829
with apt.

127
00:05:46.749 --> 00:05:51.204
First, the command sudo apt update refreshes the list of what's

128
00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:52.324
available for the machine.

129
00:05:52.349 --> 00:05:56.824
Then, sudo apt upgrade proposes the updates and waits for your

130
00:05:56.849 --> 00:05:57.164
answer.

131
00:05:57.189 --> 00:06:00.024
At the end comes the question, do you want to continue,

132
00:06:00.049 --> 00:06:03.089
with a capital Y as the default answer.

133
00:06:03.489 --> 00:06:06.544
On the practice VPS, Denise stopped at that question and read

134
00:06:06.569 --> 00:06:08.764
the whole list before answering why.

135
00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:09.764
Do the same.

136
00:06:09.789 --> 00:06:12.644
If the list shows a program you don't recognize, look it

137
00:06:12.669 --> 00:06:13.744
up before you confirm.

138
00:06:13.769 --> 00:06:16.164
And the sudo in front, as you saw in the previous

139
00:06:16.189 --> 00:06:18.974
lesson, is the request for administrator permission.

140
00:06:18.999 --> 00:06:20.699
So, what do you install?

141
00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:22.679
The course kit's bot needs Python 3.

142
00:06:24.359 --> 00:06:27.134
And git helps you carry the project from your computer to

143
00:06:27.159 --> 00:06:27.579
the VPS.

144
00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:32.239
The command is sudo apt install git python 3.

145
00:06:33.279 --> 00:06:35.934
The bot uses only what already comes with Python, with no

146
00:06:35.959 --> 00:06:37.319
extra add-ons.

147
00:06:37.359 --> 00:06:40.734
Other tools, like codecs, are optional and go in only if

148
00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:42.379
the program asks for them.

149
00:06:43.419 --> 00:06:46.554
A tutorial suggested that Lucia install 5 tools, just to be

150
00:06:46.579 --> 00:06:46.894
safe.

151
00:06:46.919 --> 00:06:49.574
She checked what the bot needed and kept 2.

152
00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:51.594
It's the carry-on bag logic.

153
00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:55.494
Every extra program is one more thing to update and maintain.

154
00:06:55.519 --> 00:06:58.574
With 5, that would be 5 things to update and 3

155
00:06:58.599 --> 00:06:59.534
sitting unused.

156
00:06:59.559 --> 00:07:03.934
With 2, both are at work and the bot loses nothing

157
00:07:03.959 --> 00:07:04.594
it does.

158
00:07:04.619 --> 00:07:07.214
Before installing, check what's already there.

159
00:07:07.239 --> 00:07:10.434
Ask each program for its version, with Python 3 and the

160
00:07:10.459 --> 00:07:12.954
version option and then with GIF and the same option.

161
00:07:12.979 --> 00:07:15.214
If it answers, it's on the machine.

162
00:07:15.239 --> 00:07:18.974
Next, create the projectiles folder in your home folder with the

163
00:07:18.999 --> 00:07:22.059
mkdir command and check it with ls.

164
00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:26.594
The tilde sign means my home folder.

165
00:07:26.619 --> 00:07:29.114
None of these commands changes the system.

166
00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:32.763
Lucia ran all of it on her laptop before renting any

167
00:07:32.788 --> 00:07:35.743
VPS and wrote down the versions.

168
00:07:35.768 --> 00:07:38.283
Pause the video and do the same in your terminal.

169
00:07:38.308 --> 00:07:40.883
On Windows use the WSL from module 3.

170
00:07:40.908 --> 00:07:44.163
If you see command not found is not your mistake.

171
00:07:44.188 --> 00:07:46.483
Note it as missing in the plan.

172
00:07:46.508 --> 00:07:50.003
Finally write in the plan what the VPS needs to get.

173
00:07:50.028 --> 00:07:53.728
Python 3, git and and nothing else, without a reason.

174
00:07:54.248 --> 00:07:57.108
With the bot inside, the front desk is still missing.

175
00:07:57.148 --> 00:08:00.688
The firewall filters network connections in both directions.

176
00:08:01.568 --> 00:08:03.283
It's like the school's front desk.

177
00:08:03.308 --> 00:08:05.843
There's a list of who can come in, and the mail

178
00:08:05.868 --> 00:08:06.663
carrier goes out to pick up the mail.

179
00:08:06.688 --> 00:08:12.043
The kids bot works the second way, with what's called long

180
00:08:12.068 --> 00:08:12.868
polling.

181
00:08:13.168 --> 00:08:16.843
It goes out through a secure connection and asks Telegram whether

182
00:08:16.868 --> 00:08:17.843
a message has arrived.

183
00:08:17.868 --> 00:08:22.243
Nobody from outside needs to knock on its door.

184
00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:24.503
Lucia thought she had to open a port so the bot

185
00:08:24.528 --> 00:08:26.028
could receive the class's messages.

186
00:08:26.848 --> 00:08:27.723
She didn't.

187
00:08:27.748 --> 00:08:30.463
Because the only one who comes into the machine is her,

188
00:08:30.488 --> 00:08:31.268
through SSH.

189
00:08:31.708 --> 00:08:32.923
So keep the rule.

190
00:08:32.948 --> 00:08:36.403
With long polling, the only entrance the VPS needs is the

191
00:08:36.428 --> 00:08:37.303
SSH one.

192
00:08:37.328 --> 00:08:39.128
Everything else can stay closed.

193
00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:43.275
On Ubuntu, the firewall is set up with UFW, and the

194
00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:44.440
order matters.

195
00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.475
Before turning it on, allow the SSH port your VPS actually

196
00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:48.840
uses.

197
00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:53.355
If it's 22, the command is sudo ufw allow 22 slash

198
00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:53.800
tcp.

199
00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.520
If it's another one, the number changes.

200
00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:02.015
Only after that comes sudo ufw enable, which turns the firewall

201
00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:02.340
on.

202
00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.515
UFW itself warns you that this may drop open SSH connections,

203
00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.120
and asks whether to proceed.

204
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.455
Denise only answered yes after setting up the ways back from

205
00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:14.800
the previous lesson.

206
00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:18.220
The second session tested and the provider's console checked.

207
00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.200
And there's one more detail.

208
00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.015
Some providers have their own firewall in the panel.

209
00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:26.515
If yours does, check there too that the SSH port is

210
00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:27.300
allowed.

211
00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:29.900
What's left is the secrets file.

212
00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:35.175
The bot's token, its password on Telegram, lives in the .env file.

213
00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.775
The command shimod600 makes that file readable only by its owner,

214
00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.945
and ls shows that at the start of the line.

215
00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:45.385
and .env never goes into git.

216
00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:48.485
The gitignore from module 4 already takes care of that.

217
00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:52.505
If the bot stops, don't open ports to see if it

218
00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:53.625
helps.

219
00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:57.205
Ask whether the process is running, whether the token was accepted

220
00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.605
and whether outgoing network works.

221
00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:03.665
The log of Denise's bot said HTTP failure 401.

222
00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.145
It was the token changed the week before.

223
00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.725
Pause the video and in a practice folder, create an empty

224
00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:13.885
dot env, lock it with cmod, and check that the line

225
00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:18.245
starts with dash, r, do, and only dashes.

226
00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:23.225
In the plan, write down the ports, the ssh1, and for

227
00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:24.650
the bot, none.

228
00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:27.685
The front desk is ready, but the bot still depends on

229
00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:28.825
you to start it.

230
00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:32.365
Up to now, you started the bot by hand, with python

231
00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:34.490
3 bot per y in the terminal.

232
00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:37.970
On the VPS, that ties the bot to your connection.

233
00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.425
Denise left the bot running in an SSH session and went

234
00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:42.025
home.

235
00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:45.185
The connection dropped on the way and the bot stopped with

236
00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:45.365
it.

237
00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:49.954
What solves this is Systemd, the machine's caretaker.

238
00:10:49.979 --> 00:10:53.054
It turns on the lights every morning, turns back on whatever

239
00:10:53.079 --> 00:10:56.774
went off and writes everything down in the incident logbook.

240
00:10:56.799 --> 00:11:01.394
With it, the bot starts by itself along with the machine,

241
00:11:01.419 --> 00:11:04.254
restarts 15 seconds after a failure and the record is kept

242
00:11:04.279 --> 00:11:07.139
for you to read later instead of vanishing with the window.

243
00:11:07.179 --> 00:11:12.314
The instruction for this caretaker is a file called a unit.

244
00:11:12.339 --> 00:11:14.599
And the kit comes with one ready.

245
00:11:14.839 --> 00:11:20.219
The kit's unit is the file oswork-bot.service.

246
00:11:20.999 --> 00:11:23.659
And it says what, with whom, and where.

247
00:11:24.199 --> 00:11:26.979
Four fields take your user and your path.

248
00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:28.119
User, who runs the bot.

249
00:11:28.619 --> 00:11:30.199
Working directory, the folder.

250
00:11:30.979 --> 00:11:33.419
Environment file, where the .env is.

251
00:11:33.679 --> 00:11:35.974
And exec start, the command that starts it.

252
00:11:35.999 --> 00:11:38.414
a fifth one you keep.

253
00:11:38.439 --> 00:11:43.554
Restart equals onFailure, which restarts it after a failure.

254
00:11:43.579 --> 00:11:47.374
On the VPS, the unit goes into the systemd folder with

255
00:11:47.399 --> 00:11:53.274
sudo, then daemon reload makes systemd reread the units, enable with

256
00:11:53.299 --> 00:11:56.564
the now option, starts the bot now and on every future

257
00:11:56.589 --> 00:11:59.189
reboot, and status shows its state.

258
00:12:00.409 --> 00:12:02.584
Denise saw active running.

259
00:12:02.609 --> 00:12:06.604
Even so, she only called the step done after sending slash

260
00:12:06.629 --> 00:12:09.369
status on Telegram and getting the answer.

261
00:12:09.969 --> 00:12:12.824
After starting it, do a restart on purpose.

262
00:12:12.849 --> 00:12:16.884
With systemd restart, send slash status again and write down the

263
00:12:16.909 --> 00:12:17.544
time.

264
00:12:17.569 --> 00:12:20.969
Next, read the record with the journalctl command.

265
00:12:21.269 --> 00:12:24.044
Lucia saw the bot stop and come back there, with date

266
00:12:24.069 --> 00:12:24.629
and time.

267
00:12:25.409 --> 00:12:28.424
But watch out, restarting doesn't fix an error that keeps coming

268
00:12:28.449 --> 00:12:29.104
back.

269
00:12:29.129 --> 00:12:33.089
On another day, she saw the line process ended for diagnosis,

270
00:12:33.289 --> 00:12:36.004
stopped the service and went looking for the cause before trying

271
00:12:36.029 --> 00:12:36.769
again.

272
00:12:36.809 --> 00:12:40.044
Now pause the video and adapt the kit's unit to your

273
00:12:40.069 --> 00:12:40.829
user.

274
00:12:40.969 --> 00:12:46.104
Unzip the kit, open oswork-bot.service with nano, replace the four

275
00:12:46.129 --> 00:12:50.524
fields with your user and path, save and check with the

276
00:12:50.549 --> 00:12:54.584
grab command that none of the four lines still says oswork.

277
00:12:54.609 --> 00:12:57.692
Without a VPS nothing starts it's just a text file.

278
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:01.020
Started and restarted still isn't care.

279
00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.875
Systemd restarts the bot, but it doesn't warn anyone if the

280
00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:06.120
bot goes silent.

281
00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.080
That's why there's the external check.

282
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:13.480
Someone outside the VPS verifies that the service answers.

283
00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:15.740
A simple daily check records two things.

284
00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:19.815
Whether the bot answered and how much disk space is left,

285
00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:21.480
which the df command shows on the VPS.

286
00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:26.455
Denise sends slash status from her phone every morning before the

287
00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:27.120
8 o'clock meeting.

288
00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.640
and writes down the time of the answer.

289
00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:32.580
Once a week, she also writes down the disk space.

290
00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.435
If your phone gets the answer, the whole path works.

291
00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.655
As for the disk, you look at it before it fills

292
00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:42.255
up, because a full disk makes any service stop, and the

293
00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:45.600
node carries a name, because the check belongs to someone.

294
00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.655
Now, the backups.

295
00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.520
Keep copies of the data outside the VPS.

296
00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:54.795
For example, in a protected folder in the school's drive, or

297
00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:55.955
on an external disk.

298
00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.600
If the machine disappears, the copy can't go with it.

299
00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:03.380
Also set the retention, meaning how long each copy is kept.

300
00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:06.315
Lucia used to keep the copy on the same VPS and

301
00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:09.780
started keeping it outside, with three monthly copies.

302
00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:13.035
The .env, with the token, was left out.

303
00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:15.855
But a copy only counts after the rehearsal, like a fire

304
00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:16.515
drill.

305
00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:20.695
The test restores the file vendas.csv into a separate folder and

306
00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:23.220
compares it with the original using diff.

307
00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.955
If difficile says nothing, the two are identical.

308
00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:28.775
In Denise's first test, the copy was empty.

309
00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:31.215
The command pointed to the wrong folder.

310
00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.600
The rehearsal found the error before a real loss.

311
00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:38.200
The course project closes with five pieces of evidence.

312
00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:42.415
Authorize the response, unknown user blocked, restart, log without

313
00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:44.568
token, and restore checked.

314
00:14:44.593 --> 00:14:49.928
Without a second telegram account, the unknown user block can be

315
00:14:49.953 --> 00:14:52.368
proven with the kit's self-test.

316
00:14:52.393 --> 00:14:55.128
And whatever wasn't done, you declare.

317
00:14:55.153 --> 00:14:58.508
Denise didn't have the VPS yet, so on the restart line

318
00:14:58.533 --> 00:15:01.908
she wrote not executed, no VPS.

319
00:15:01.933 --> 00:15:05.748
A written not done is worth more than a done that

320
00:15:05.773 --> 00:15:06.813
nobody checked.

321
00:15:07.113 --> 00:15:10.508
Now, pause the video and make a real backup on your

322
00:15:10.533 --> 00:15:11.393
computer.

323
00:15:11.653 --> 00:15:16.648
Copy the kit's vendas.csv into a backup folder, restore it into

324
00:15:16.673 --> 00:15:18.693
a separate folder, and run diff.

325
00:15:19.053 --> 00:15:21.053
Silence is the sign you got it right.

326
00:15:21.973 --> 00:15:24.988
Then show the file with cat and add up the values.

327
00:15:25.013 --> 00:15:26.073
The total has to be 100.

328
00:15:26.773 --> 00:15:28.988
Write the date and the result in the plan.

329
00:15:29.013 --> 00:15:32.453
To wrap up, a VPS is a rented room.

330
00:15:32.553 --> 00:15:35.388
It runs all the time and you're the one who takes

331
00:15:35.413 --> 00:15:36.353
care of it.

332
00:15:36.473 --> 00:15:40.108
size follows the program, each bill has its limit, and the

333
00:15:40.133 --> 00:15:41.948
plan comes before the purchase.

334
00:15:41.973 --> 00:15:45.148
You get in through SSH, keeping the old key until the

335
00:15:45.173 --> 00:15:48.668
new one works, install only what the bot asks for, leave

336
00:15:48.693 --> 00:15:53.408
only the SSH port open, lock the dot env, and hand

337
00:15:53.433 --> 00:15:57.368
the bot to systemd, which starts, restarts, and logs.

338
00:15:57.393 --> 00:16:01.408
And you prove from outside that it answers, and you rehearse

339
00:16:01.433 --> 00:16:02.028
the restore.

340
00:16:02.053 --> 00:16:05.128
With that, OS work comes to an end.

341
00:16:05.153 --> 00:16:09.528
You leave with an organized folder with verifiable instructions, a

342
00:16:09.553 --> 00:16:13.528
skill, the history in Git, a restricted bot, and a supervised

343
00:16:13.553 --> 00:16:15.268
operation plan on a VPS.

344
00:16:15.293 --> 00:16:17.988
From here on, the course becomes routine.

345
00:16:18.013 --> 00:16:21.788
The daily check, the monthly rehearsal, and the plan updated with

346
00:16:21.813 --> 00:16:23.368
every change.

347
00:16:23.393 --> 00:16:26.328
Put the next Restart test on your calendar now, a month

348
00:16:26.353 --> 00:16:27.048
from today.

349
00:16:27.073 --> 00:16:29.028
Thank you for making it this far.
